From: monish.v <monish.v>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: take the NPU tx token count from the NPU
 driver

mt7996_npu_offload_init() tells the NPU firmware how many tx tokens it may
use with the compile time MT7996_HW_TOKEN_SIZE. A token is an index into
the "tx-pkt" reserved memory region (token i is at base + i * 2KB), so that
constant has to agree with the size of a reservation it knows nothing about:
too large and the NPU writes past the end of the region into memory owned by
Linux.

The npu driver now reads the count from the device tree
(airoha,tx-token-num on the npu node), checks it against the reservation and
exports it as npu->tx_token_num. Use that, so the token count and the DRAM
it indexes are described in exactly one place.

Fall back to MT7996_HW_TOKEN_SIZE when the field is zero, i.e. when running
against a device tree that does not carry the count.

mt7996_wed_init() keeps using MT7996_HW_TOKEN_SIZE: WED is a different
offload engine with its own buffers and is not used on the AN7581.

Signed-off-by: monish.v <monish.v>
---
 mt7996/npu.c |    5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/mt7996/npu.c
+++ b/mt7996/npu.c
@@ -200,6 +200,7 @@ static int mt7996_npu_txrx_offload_init(
 static int mt7996_npu_offload_init(struct mt7996_dev *dev,
 				   struct airoha_npu *npu)
 {
+	u32 token_size = npu->tx_token_num ?: MT7996_HW_TOKEN_SIZE;
 	u32 val;
 	int err;
 
@@ -230,11 +231,11 @@ static int mt7996_npu_offload_init(struc
 		return err;
 
 	err = mt76_npu_send_msg(npu, 0, WLAN_FUNC_SET_WAIT_TOKEN_ID_SIZE,
-				MT7996_HW_TOKEN_SIZE, GFP_KERNEL);
+				token_size, GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
-	dev->mt76.token_start = MT7996_HW_TOKEN_SIZE;
+	dev->mt76.token_start = token_size;
 
 	return 0;
 }
